// AUTHORED BATCH 004 — B1, B2 and C1, neutral-academic register.
//
// Companion batch to 003, same distractor policy: prefer wrong options drawn
// from the sentence's own subject area (other properties of water, other
// features of a building, other kinds of objection) over stock absurd nouns,
// so ruling a distractor out requires reading the whole sentence.
//
// STATUS: stage 3 only. Not blind-checked, not shippable.
{"target_word": "worthwhile", "cefr": "B1", "sentence": "Most participants felt the extra hours of study had been ______ once the results appeared.", "correct_answer": "worthwhile", "distractors": ["identical", "confidential", "circular"], "explanation": "Effort that turns out to have been worth making is 'worthwhile', which is what good results show. Hours of study cannot be identical to nothing in particular, confidential, or circular.", "difficulty_est": 3}
{"target_word": "logic", "cefr": "B1", "sentence": "The argument fails not because the evidence is weak but because its ______ is faulty.", "correct_answer": "logic", "distractors": ["binding", "packaging", "lighting"], "explanation": "The way reasoning moves from evidence to conclusion is its 'logic', and that is the alternative fault the sentence offers. Binding, packaging and lighting belong to physical objects, not to an argument.", "difficulty_est": 3}
{"target_word": "definite", "cefr": "B1", "sentence": "There is no ______ answer yet, since the last set of results has not arrived.", "correct_answer": "definite", "distractors": ["muscular", "orchestral", "granular"], "explanation": "An answer that is certain and settled is 'definite', and missing results are exactly what prevent certainty. An answer cannot be muscular, orchestral or granular.", "difficulty_est": 3}
{"target_word": "intense", "cefr": "B1", "sentence": "The heat inside the furnace is so ______ that the sensors must be replaced monthly.", "correct_answer": "intense", "distractors": ["fictional", "polite", "rural"], "explanation": "Heat strong enough to destroy equipment every month is 'intense'. Heat is a physical condition and cannot be fictional, polite or rural.", "difficulty_est": 2}
{"target_word": "usage", "cefr": "B1", "sentence": "Water ______ in the city fell by a fifth after the new pricing scheme began.", "correct_answer": "usage", "distractors": ["temperature", "hardness", "clarity"], "explanation": "How much water people consume is 'usage', and that is the one thing a change in price would affect. Temperature, hardness and clarity are real properties of water, but none of them responds to a pricing scheme.", "difficulty_est": 3}
{"target_word": "storage", "cefr": "B1", "sentence": "The building has almost no ______, so equipment is left standing in the corridors overnight.", "correct_answer": "storage", "distractors": ["ventilation", "signage", "insulation"], "explanation": "Space to put things away is 'storage', and its absence is what leaves equipment in the corridors. Ventilation, signage and insulation are also features a building may lack, but none of them would force equipment into a corridor.", "difficulty_est": 3}
{"target_word": "height", "cefr": "B1", "sentence": "The ______ of the wall was reduced so that residents behind it could see the river.", "correct_answer": "height", "distractors": ["thickness", "weight", "cost"], "explanation": "Only lowering how tall the wall is lets people see over it, and that measurement is its 'height'. Reducing the wall's thickness, weight or cost would leave the view blocked exactly as before.", "difficulty_est": 3}
{"target_word": "prove", "cefr": "B1", "sentence": "A single counter-example is enough to ______ that the rule cannot hold in every case.", "correct_answer": "prove", "distractors": ["forget", "promise", "regret"], "explanation": "Evidence that settles a question beyond doubt is enough to 'prove' the point. A counter-example cannot forget, promise or regret anything, since those describe what people do.", "difficulty_est": 3}
{"target_word": "triumph", "cefr": "B1", "sentence": "The expedition was regarded as a ______ despite the loss of most of its equipment.", "correct_answer": "triumph", "distractors": ["formality", "coincidence", "postponement"], "explanation": "A great success achieved in spite of difficulty is a 'triumph', which the word 'despite' sets up. Losing equipment does not make an expedition a formality, a coincidence or a postponement.", "difficulty_est": 3}
{"target_word": "discrimination", "cefr": "B1", "sentence": "The report found clear evidence of ______ against older applicants at every stage of hiring.", "correct_answer": "discrimination", "distractors": ["gratitude", "flattery", "generosity"], "explanation": "Treating one group worse because of who they are is 'discrimination', and the word 'against' points to unfair treatment. Gratitude, flattery and generosity are all favourable, so none of them can be directed against applicants.", "difficulty_est": 3}
{"target_word": "typical", "cefr": "B1", "sentence": "A ______ working day at the station involves three hours of observation and four of analysis.", "correct_answer": "typical", "distractors": ["reversible", "hollow", "fluent"], "explanation": "A day that shows the usual pattern is a 'typical' one, which is why the hours can be listed. A working day cannot be reversible, hollow or fluent.", "difficulty_est": 3}
{"target_word": "gesture", "cefr": "B1", "sentence": "Offering to pay for the repairs was a generous ______ that the neighbours did not expect.", "correct_answer": "gesture", "distractors": ["receipt", "estimate", "invoice"], "explanation": "An action done to show goodwill is a 'gesture'. A receipt, an estimate and an invoice are documents produced in the course of paying for repairs, and a document cannot itself be generous.", "difficulty_est": 3}
{"target_word": "routine", "cefr": "B1", "sentence": "Feeding the animals at the same hour each day quickly became an unbreakable ______.", "correct_answer": "routine", "distractors": ["alibi", "promise", "silence"], "explanation": "A fixed pattern repeated at the same time every day is a 'routine'. An alibi, a promise and a silence are not repeated activities, so none of them is what daily feeding becomes.", "difficulty_est": 3}
{"target_word": "manufacturer", "cefr": "B2", "sentence": "The ______ recalled every unit sold that year after a fault appeared in the wiring.", "correct_answer": "manufacturer", "distractors": ["spectator", "librarian", "biographer"], "explanation": "The company that builds a product is the 'manufacturer', and only it can recall what it sold. A spectator, a librarian and a biographer have no connection with producing or withdrawing wired units.", "difficulty_est": 3}
{"target_word": "allegation", "cefr": "B2", "sentence": "The ______ against the treasurer was investigated for six months and finally dismissed for lack of evidence.", "correct_answer": "allegation", "distractors": ["nomination", "invitation", "renovation"], "explanation": "An unproved claim that someone has done wrong is an 'allegation', which is what gets investigated and then dismissed. A nomination, an invitation and a renovation cannot be made against a person.", "difficulty_est": 4}
{"target_word": "reaction", "cefr": "B2", "sentence": "Public ______ to the new proposal was far angrier than the ministry had ever anticipated.", "correct_answer": "reaction", "distractors": ["obedience", "attendance", "subscription"], "explanation": "The way people respond to something is their 'reaction', and only a response can be described as angry. Obedience, attendance and subscription are forms of cooperation and cannot carry anger.", "difficulty_est": 3}
{"target_word": "prominence", "cefr": "B2", "sentence": "The little-known theory gained sudden ______ after two independent laboratories confirmed its central prediction.", "correct_answer": "prominence", "distractors": ["fluency", "humidity", "elasticity"], "explanation": "Becoming widely noticed and important is gaining 'prominence', which contrasts with 'little-known'. A theory has no fluency, humidity or elasticity to gain.", "difficulty_est": 4}
{"target_word": "symbolic", "cefr": "B2", "sentence": "The rent was ______ rather than real, amounting to one coin handed to the landlord each year.", "correct_answer": "symbolic", "distractors": ["electronic", "decorative", "musical"], "explanation": "A payment that stands for something instead of covering a real cost is 'symbolic', as a single coin shows. A rent cannot be electronic in contrast to real, and it cannot be decorative or musical at all.", "difficulty_est": 4}
{"target_word": "dynamic", "cefr": "B2", "sentence": "Rather than a fixed list, the system uses a ______ model that updates as new data arrives.", "correct_answer": "dynamic", "distractors": ["printed", "wooden", "framed"], "explanation": "A model that keeps changing in response to new information is 'dynamic', which is the opposite of the fixed list. Something printed, wooden or framed is by nature unable to update itself.", "difficulty_est": 4}
{"target_word": "recruit", "cefr": "B2", "sentence": "The laboratory struggled to ______ enough volunteers for a trial lasting three whole years.", "correct_answer": "recruit", "distractors": ["photograph", "impersonate", "congratulate"], "explanation": "Persuading people to join a study is to 'recruit' them, and a long trial makes that hard. Photographing, impersonating or congratulating volunteers would not fill the places on a trial.", "difficulty_est": 4}
{"target_word": "prosecute", "cefr": "B2", "sentence": "The environmental agency can ______ any company that knowingly conceals a spill from the regulator.", "correct_answer": "prosecute", "distractors": ["reimburse", "advertise", "relocate"], "explanation": "Taking a wrongdoer to court is to 'prosecute' them, which is the power an agency holds over concealment. Reimbursing, advertising or relocating a company is not a penalty for hiding a spill.", "difficulty_est": 4}
{"target_word": "starvation", "cefr": "B2", "sentence": "Whole districts faced ______ that winter when the harvest failed for a third successive year.", "correct_answer": "starvation", "distractors": ["taxation", "conscription", "quarantine"], "explanation": "Having no food to eat after repeated crop failure is 'starvation'. Taxation, conscription and quarantine are all things a population can face, but none of them follows from a failed harvest.", "difficulty_est": 4}
{"target_word": "fraud", "cefr": "B2", "sentence": "Two managers were dismissed for ______ after auditors traced invoices to companies that did not exist.", "correct_answer": "fraud", "distractors": ["lateness", "absence", "plagiarism"], "explanation": "Inventing companies in order to issue false invoices is deliberate deception for money, which 'fraud' names. Lateness, absence and plagiarism are also grounds for dismissal, but none of them involves fake invoices.", "difficulty_est": 4}
{"target_word": "evaluation", "cefr": "B2", "sentence": "A proper ______ of the programme will take at least two years of careful measurement.", "correct_answer": "evaluation", "distractors": ["celebration", "abbreviation", "illustration"], "explanation": "Judging how well something works, using measurement, is an 'evaluation' of it. A celebration, an abbreviation and an illustration require no measurement and would not take two years.", "difficulty_est": 4}
{"target_word": "catastrophe", "cefr": "B2", "sentence": "What began as a minor leak became a ______ that closed the plant for two years.", "correct_answer": "catastrophe", "distractors": ["curiosity", "tradition", "bargain"], "explanation": "A disaster serious enough to shut a plant for two years is a 'catastrophe', which contrasts with 'minor leak'. A curiosity, a tradition and a bargain would give nobody a reason to close anything.", "difficulty_est": 4}
{"target_word": "flawless", "cefr": "B2", "sentence": "Her performance of the difficult passage was ______, and the recording needed no editing at all.", "correct_answer": "flawless", "distractors": ["inaudible", "unfinished", "shortened"], "explanation": "A performance with no mistakes in it is 'flawless', which is why no editing was needed. A recording that was inaudible, unfinished or shortened would need more work, not less.", "difficulty_est": 4}
{"target_word": "utility", "cefr": "B2", "sentence": "A tool with no ______ will not sell, however beautiful its designer has managed to make it.", "correct_answer": "utility", "distractors": ["fragrance", "vocabulary", "rhythm"], "explanation": "Usefulness is what a tool has to have, and 'utility' names it; the contrast with beauty confirms this. Nobody buys or refuses a tool on the basis of its fragrance, vocabulary or rhythm.", "difficulty_est": 5}
{"target_word": "conformity", "cefr": "C1", "sentence": "Strict ______ to the house style was expected of every contributor to the new journal.", "correct_answer": "conformity", "distractors": ["objection", "indifference", "exposure"], "explanation": "Following a set of rules exactly is 'conformity' to them, which is what a house style demands. A journal would not require strict objection, indifference or exposure to its own style.", "difficulty_est": 5}
{"target_word": "cynical", "cefr": "C1", "sentence": "He had grown so ______ that he assumed every act of generosity concealed a private motive.", "correct_answer": "cynical", "distractors": ["forgetful", "sentimental", "prosperous"], "explanation": "Believing that people always act from selfish motives is being 'cynical'. Someone forgetful, sentimental or prosperous would have no particular reason to suspect hidden motives behind generosity.", "difficulty_est": 5}
{"target_word": "superficial", "cefr": "C1", "sentence": "Her knowledge of the subject proved ______, collapsing as soon as anyone asked a detailed question.", "correct_answer": "superficial", "distractors": ["hereditary", "confidential", "encyclopaedic"], "explanation": "Knowledge that goes no deeper than the surface is 'superficial', which is why detail destroys it. Encyclopaedic knowledge would survive detailed questions, and knowledge that failed under questioning is not shown to be hereditary or confidential.", "difficulty_est": 5}
{"target_word": "impede", "cefr": "C1", "sentence": "Thick fog continued to ______ the rescue effort for several hours after the accident.", "correct_answer": "impede", "distractors": ["illuminate", "finance", "commemorate"], "explanation": "Getting in the way of an operation and slowing it down is to 'impede' it, which is what fog does. Fog cannot illuminate, finance or commemorate a rescue.", "difficulty_est": 5}
{"target_word": "likelihood", "cefr": "C1", "sentence": "The ______ of a second failure rises sharply once a component has been repaired twice.", "correct_answer": "likelihood", "distractors": ["colour", "symmetry", "texture"], "explanation": "How probable something is constitutes its 'likelihood', and repeated repairs make a further breakdown more probable. A failure has no colour, symmetry or texture that could rise.", "difficulty_est": 5}
{"target_word": "notable", "cefr": "C1", "sentence": "One ______ exception is the desert species, which survives without drinking any water at all.", "correct_answer": "notable", "distractors": ["numerical", "grammatical", "hourly"], "explanation": "An exception striking enough to be worth mentioning is a 'notable' one. An animal that never drinks is not a numerical, grammatical or hourly exception to anything.", "difficulty_est": 4}
{"target_word": "inheritance", "cefr": "C1", "sentence": "Most of the land came to her as an ______ rather than through any purchase of her own.", "correct_answer": "inheritance", "distractors": ["apology", "afterthought", "achievement"], "explanation": "Property received from someone who has died is an 'inheritance', which is the alternative to buying it. Land does not arrive as an apology, an afterthought or an achievement.", "difficulty_est": 5}
{"target_word": "evoke", "cefr": "C1", "sentence": "Certain smells can ______ a childhood memory more sharply than any photograph ever manages to.", "correct_answer": "evoke", "distractors": ["decorate", "purchase", "rehearse"], "explanation": "Bringing a memory vividly to mind is what 'evoke' means, and the comparison with photographs confirms it. A smell cannot decorate, purchase or rehearse a memory.", "difficulty_est": 5}
{"target_word": "degrade", "cefr": "C1", "sentence": "Direct sunlight will slowly ______ the plastic until it becomes brittle and breaks apart completely.", "correct_answer": "degrade", "distractors": ["polish", "magnetise", "laminate"], "explanation": "Breaking a material down over time so that it weakens is to 'degrade' it, which the brittleness demonstrates. Polishing, magnetising or laminating plastic would not leave it falling to pieces.", "difficulty_est": 5}
{"target_word": "scenario", "cefr": "C1", "sentence": "Planners tested a worst-case ______ in which every supplier failed during the same week.", "correct_answer": "scenario", "distractors": ["ornament", "inventory", "portrait"], "explanation": "An imagined sequence of events used for planning is a 'scenario', and the relative clause describes one. An ornament, an inventory and a portrait are objects or lists, and events cannot happen inside them.", "difficulty_est": 5}
{"target_word": "vitality", "cefr": "C1", "sentence": "The old quarter has recovered much of its ______, with new shops opening every month.", "correct_answer": "vitality", "distractors": ["silence", "obscurity", "flatness"], "explanation": "Energy and liveliness are 'vitality', which new shops every month demonstrate. Recovering silence, obscurity or flatness would mean the opposite of a district coming back to life.", "difficulty_est": 5}
{"target_word": "align", "cefr": "C1", "sentence": "Engineers had to ______ the two halves of the tunnel to within a few millimetres.", "correct_answer": "align", "distractors": ["insulate", "ventilate", "flood"], "explanation": "Bringing two parts into exactly the right position relative to each other is to 'align' them, which the millimetre tolerance requires. Insulating, ventilating and flooding a tunnel are not measured as a matter of alignment.", "difficulty_est": 5}
{"target_word": "aesthetic", "cefr": "C1", "sentence": "The objection was purely ______: the bridge worked perfectly but many residents found it ugly.", "correct_answer": "aesthetic", "distractors": ["financial", "legal", "technical"], "explanation": "An objection about how something looks is an 'aesthetic' one, which the word 'ugly' makes explicit. The bridge working perfectly rules out a technical complaint, and nothing in the sentence concerns money or law.", "difficulty_est": 5}
